comments:
@beldmit told me (privately) that he patched OpenSSL such that both thread-local DRBG instances reseed with prediction resistance enabled (when their threshold has been reached or their reseed counter differs with the counter of the primary DRBG). Mixing calls to those two DRBGs introduces the same negative side-effects, namely that after the first reseeding the reseed counters of those two instances will be out-of-sync, so you get a full reseeding on every generate call.

```C
    for(;;) {
        RAND_bytes(...);
        RAND_priv_bytes(...);
    }
```
IMHO I would enforce prediction resistance only when reseeding the private instance, not the public one, since the former is intended for long-term secrets, while the other is for arbitrary randomness (e.g. nonces). That was the whole point of having two instances in the first place.
